# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
# Part 3: discussion, conclusions, declarations and appendices.

add(("h1", "5. Discussion"))
add(("h2", "5.1 What the simulations establish, and what they assume"))
add(("p", r"Every quantitative statement of Section 4 is a property of the model of Section 3 under the sampling choices of Table 3. Table 9 separates what was assumed, what follows from the equations or from the simulations as a consequence of those assumptions, what is only proposed as a stand-in for a measurement, and what the study does not address. The distinction matters most for the fractions of Section 4.5: a fraction of sampled parameterizations that show an interior optimum describes the sampling design, and is neither the probability that such an optimum exists in human physiology nor evidence about the parameters of any individual."))
add(("table", r"Table 9. Assumed, derived, proposed and unaddressed elements.",
     ["Class", "Elements"],
     [["Assumed (structure)", r"Quasi-steady glucose, Eq. (1); a scalar latent information loss $I$ that is posited, not measured; the Hill form of drug-induced lean-mass loss and the power form of the muscle-deficit cost (varied in Section 4.7); constant dose from age 45; a storage set point that rises with $I$; no individual variability"],
      ["Assumed (numbers)", "All parameter values of Table 1; they reproduce three qualitative targets of the untreated course and were not fitted"],
      ["Derived within the model", r"The exact pathway decomposition, Eq. (10); $J'(0) < 0$ for $n_H > 1$; the quasi-static stationarity condition, Eq. (11); the existence fractions, their dependence on prior, horizon and ablation; the Sobol indices; the information gains of Section 4.11"],
      ["Proposed, not validated", "SIM as a surrogate for the glucose-to-muscle balance; metabolic age as a readout; the candidate observable panel of Section 5.3"],
      ["Not addressed", "Reversal of information loss; the biology of any particular agent; dosing in people; heterogeneity between tissues or individuals; fast glucose dynamics and postprandial excursions; causal identification from observational data"]],
     [1.6, 7.2]))
add(("h2", "5.2 Interpretation"))
add(("p", r"The model gives a specific reading of the trade-off. A glucose-lowering agent has two benefits, one through glucose and one through storage, and one cost, through muscle. The cost is negligible at small dose, because the sigmoidal muscle response starts slowly, and then outgrows the benefits (Fig. 3B). The location of the optimum is set mainly by the maximum fractional muscle loss, the glucose and muscle couplings, the weight of muscle in glucose disposal and the shape exponents of the muscle response and cost, and not by the baseline turnover or by the relaxation rates of the compartments (Fig. 8). In the quasi-static limit an interior optimum requires the muscle coupling to exceed about the glucose coupling (Section 4.4), and in the full model it also requires a muscle-loss response that starts slowly at small dose (Section 4.7). Two consequences are testable in principle. The optimal dose moves up when the muscle loss per unit dose is reduced, for example by a co-intervention that preserves muscle, such as resistance exercise combined with weight loss in older adults [@Villareal2011] or an agent that increases lean mass [@Heymsfield2021]. And an intervention that is judged by its effect on glucose alone cannot detect the optimum, because the benefit through the glucose and storage channels increases with dose throughout the range studied."))
add(("p", r"The adiposity result is a statement about the structure of the model and not about adipose biology. Because the agent reduces the storage compartment, an adiposity penalty adds a benefit that is already largest at small dose, so it deepens the benefit side without changing the muscle cost. In this model it does not remove the interior optimum in any configuration examined; it moves the nominal optimum by less than 0.02 in dose and deepens the benefit by a factor of {FATFACTOR}, and these sizes are sensitivities of the model to an unmeasured coupling, not estimates."))
add(("h2", "5.3 SIM: theoretical definition, candidate observable and validation"))
add(("p", r"SIM is useful in the model as a derived readout whose log-derivative decomposes into the proportional changes of glucose, susceptibility and muscle. It should not be read as an aging clock. Section 4.3 shows that, in the model, the metabolic age computed from SIM overstates the benefit relative to the metabolic age computed from the information-loss variable, and that the two have different optimal doses, because SIM contains the direct glucose-lowering effect of the agent, which acts on glucose instantly and on information loss only through the damage rate. Table 10 therefore distinguishes three levels. Only the first exists in this study."))
add(("table", r"Table 10. Three levels of the SIM index. A candidate panel is a proposal for a future study; no formula or coefficient in the second row has been estimated from data.",
     ["Level", "Definition", "Status"],
     [["SIM-theoretical", r"$G\,\sigma(I)/M$ with the latent $I$ in $\sigma$; Eq. (7)", "Defined and analyzed in this study; not computable from a blood panel because $I$ is latent"],
      ["SIM-observable (candidate)", r"A ratio of a reference-normalized glycemic term (fasting glucose or glycated hemoglobin) multiplied by a susceptibility term from an inflammatory marker (high-sensitivity C-reactive protein) to a muscle term (appendicular lean mass indexed to height squared, or a creatinine-to-cystatin C ratio). The form of the susceptibility term and its coefficient must be estimated, not assumed. Pre-specified rules are needed for units, log transformation, sex- and age-specific reference values, exclusion of acute illness for the inflammatory marker, and adjustment for renal function, for the muscle term, and for the use of glucose-lowering medication, which lowers the glycemic term directly", "Proposal only; not specified numerically and not validated"],
      ["SIM-validation", "Comparison of the observable index and of its change under treatment with a methylation-based clock or entropy measure in longitudinal data, with the metabolic age of Eq. (8) computed for each; success and failure criteria in Table 11", "Not done"]],
     [1.7, 5.2, 2.2]))
add(("p", r"Candidate components exist in the literature: the triglyceride–glucose index is a surrogate for insulin resistance [@Simental2008] that has been reported to predict mortality in diabetes [@Zhao2023]; the methylation-based clocks [@Horvath2013;@Levine2018] and entropy measures [@Chan2025] would supply the reference against which an observable SIM must be validated; and clock-based endpoints have been used in small trials [@Fahy2019]. None of these studies tested the proposed ratio, and no claim is made that SIM tracks a methylation clock."))
add(("h2", "5.4 Relation to existing work"))
add(("p", r"The model complements rather than competes with epigenetic clocks. A clock estimates the state from data; this model proposes a mechanism for how a state variable would respond to a dose, so that a clock-based measurement could test it. Compared with models of glucose–insulin dynamics [@Bergman1979;@Topp2000] and body-weight dynamics [@Hall2011], it is far less detailed on the physiology of glucose and weight, and it adds the coupling to a muscle reserve and to a latent aging variable. The existence of an interior optimum in a trade-off between a benefit that saturates early and a cost that saturates late is a generic feature of dose–response relations; what the model adds is that the position of the optimum is a ratio of two couplings and that the ratio can in principle be measured. The conceptual background is the older idea that aging reflects the allocation of resources between maintenance and other functions [@Kirkwood1977]."))
add(("h2", "5.5 A falsifiable experiment and the outcomes that would refute the model"))
add(("p", r"The model is falsifiable by a dose-ranging study in which a readout of information loss, lean mass, storage and glucose are measured in the same subjects at several doses. Table 11 states the predictions, the observation that tests each one and the result that would count against the model. In every case the comparison is with the measurement error of the readout, which the model does not supply."))
add(("table", r"Table 11. Predictions of the model and the outcomes that would refute them.",
     ["Prediction", "Observation", "Refuting outcome"],
     [["A U-shaped dose–response of information loss", "A methylation-entropy or clock readout at the horizon, at four or more doses that span a range of lean-mass loss", "A monotone decrease of the readout up to doses that remove a large fraction of lean mass"],
      ["The optimum moves up with a muscle-preserving co-intervention", "The same dose-ranging design with and without resistance exercise or an agent that increases lean mass", "No shift of the minimum, or a shift down"],
      ["An interior optimum requires a muscle coupling comparable to the glucose coupling (Eq. (11))", "Regression of the rate of change of a clock readout on a muscle deficit and on glucose in a longitudinal cohort, to estimate the ratio of the two couplings", "A ratio far below that of Section 4.4 together with a U-shaped dose–response"],
      ["SIM overstates the benefit relative to a direct readout of information loss (Section 4.3)", "Change in SIM and in the clock readout under the same treatment", "A change in SIM that is not larger than the change in the clock readout"],
      ["The muscle coupling that sets the optimum is constrained only by dose-ranging data with a direct readout (Section 4.11)", "Estimation of the muscle coupling from one dose versus from two or more doses with the clock readout", "A precise estimate from untreated and single-dose cohorts alone"]],
     [3.0, 3.4, 2.6]))
add(("h2", "5.6 Limitations"))
add(("p", r"The model is low-dimensional and mean-field: heterogeneous tissues are compressed into three scalars and the information loss into a single variable, and alternatives such as a vector of tissue-specific information loss, explicit fast glucose dynamics, a non-linear susceptibility or a different saturating muscle response were not examined beyond the functional-form substitutions of Section 4.7. The parameters are illustrative and the analysis is conditional on them. The stationarity condition of Eq. (11) is a quasi-static approximation to the dynamic problem and is not a sufficient condition for an interior minimum of $J$; its accuracy is tested numerically and not proved. The identifiability analysis is local, at the nominal point, with assumed observation sets and noise levels. The dose is a single constant and the agent is not specified, so the results do not describe the pharmacology of any drug, and nothing in them is a recommendation about dosing, which would require individual data and clinical judgment. Correlation cannot be separated from causation within a simulation. Finally, a coupling parameter that interpolated between compartmentalized and integrated organisms in an earlier formulation, and the comparison with long-lived plants that it supported, are outside the scope of this study and are not used."))

add(("h1", "6. Conclusions"))
add(("p", r"In a minimal dynamical model of metabolic aging, a glucose-lowering intervention that also removes lean mass has an interior optimal dose when the muscle-deficit coupling of the aging rate is comparable to or larger than the glucose coupling. The slope of the dose–response splits exactly into a glucose, a storage and a muscle pathway, the quasi-static stationarity condition is a ratio of two couplings, and the existence of the optimum is robust to the sampling prior and to the removal of the direct glucose effect, the susceptibility feedback, the information-to-glucose feedback or the storage channel. It depends on the muscle-deficit cost, on drug-induced muscle loss, on the role of muscle in glucose disposal and on a muscle-loss response that starts slowly at small dose, and it depends on the treatment horizon, being present in about a quarter of parameterizations after 5 years and in more than half after 30. An adiposity penalty deepens the benefit and moves the nominal optimum by less than 0.02 in dose without removing it. The index SIM, computed from the model states, overstates the benefit relative to the information-loss variable and has a different optimum, so it is a candidate surrogate and not a clock. The ratio of couplings that governs the optimum is only weakly constrained by glucose, muscle and storage measurements on untreated and single-dose cohorts, and even a dose-ranging design with a direct information-loss readout constrains it only partly. These are conditional, model-based statements that define a validation agenda and a set of refuting outcomes; they are not evidence about human aging or a basis for dosing."))

